  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe pattern becomes remains active over the next week with \npersistent longwave troughiness over the region and several \nshortwaves and strong jet energy. Intermittent low rain chances will \nstick around throughout the week, but the greatest chances concern a \nsystem anticipated late Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\nThe system will take shape late Sunday into Monday as a shortwave \ndigs from over the Pacific NW and phases with subtropical jet energy \nto aid in the lee cyclogenesis off the Rockies. The corresponding \nsurface low will rapidly deepen as it tracks across the great \nplains. This should create breezy conditions Monday as the pressure \ngradient tightens in response. Moisture return in response will push \ndewpoints up into the upper 60s near 70 ahead of its attendant cold \nfront. The cold front should lag behind the surface slow so most low \nlevel forcing will be focused north. Storms will develop along the \nfront west over Oklahoma and Texas and push into our area late \nTuesday as the front approaches. Lightning probabilities continue to \nsuggest weakening with eastward extent which makes sense as low \nlevel forcing weakens. This all acts to slow the front down and the \nstorms along it will reside in an environment favorable for heavy \nrainfall with PWATs approaching 2 inches and moist convergence. \nStorm motions suggest training along the slow moving boundary. \nRainfall amounts can easily stack up in this scenario. Some guidance \nis suggesting bullseyes anywhere between 4 and 7 inches in a short \nperiod. This threat will have to continue to be monitored.\n\nThere is a low end chance for isolated severe weather as well with \nthis system, but secondary to flash flood threat. Linear storm mode \nand high shear low cape type environment suggest a primary \nwind/tornado risk, which would likely be isolated with greatest \nchances west of I-55 and the river. Heavy rainfall, as well as \novernight timing, may dwarf this threat a bit however and limit \ninstability. Still, it is worth monitoring.\n\nDue to the secondary low development into Wednesday, moisture wrap \naround lingers, so low (15-20%) rain chances continue through \nThursday. Additional rainfall amounts to generally under a tenth of \nan inch. The jet remains active and persistent moist trajectories \nmay keep isolated rain chances around to end the week into next \nweekend. /SAS/",
